---
/**
 * ABOUT PAGE — supporting trust content. Even here, open by talking to
 * the visitor about what the business does FOR them; history appears
 * only as brief supporting proof (Rule 22), never the opener.
 *
 * CONROE TREE CO.: no named people, credentials, or photos are claimed
 * until they are real. Trust here comes from how the work is quoted and
 * done, plus honest consumer guidance. FILL-IN when the operating crew
 * is signed: real team photos (descriptive filenames + specific alt
 * text, Rule 26), any real credentials, and the crew's own story.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CTA from '../components/CTA.astro';
import MapNAP from '../components/MapNAP.astro';
import { business as b, cityState } from '../config/business';

const title = `About ${b.name} | ${b.primaryCategory} in ${cityState}`;
const description = `How ${b.name} works: written estimates before any cut, ANSI A300 trimming practices, and full cleanup on every Conroe, TX tree job. See what to ask any tree service.`;
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>The Way Tree Work Should Be Done in {b.address.city}</h1>
      <p class="hero__sub">Written estimates, controlled removals, and a yard that looks better when the trucks leave than it did when they showed up.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>What You Are Hiring</h2>
      <p>
        When a 90 foot loblolly is leaning over your roof, you are not really
        buying a chainsaw. You are buying judgment: which limbs come off first,
        where each log lands, and whether the tree even needs to come down at
        all. That judgment is the product here. Every job in {b.address.city}
        starts with someone walking the property with you, saying out loud what
        the tree is doing and what it will cost to fix, and putting that number
        in writing before a single saw starts.
      </p>
      <p>
        The work itself covers the full list a Montgomery County property
        throws at you: <a href="/services/tree-removal/">removals</a> from
        easy-access front yards to pines threaded between houses,
        {' '}<a href="/services/tree-trimming/">trimming and pruning</a> to
        ANSI A300 practices, <a href="/services/stump-grinding/">stump
        grinding</a>, <a href="/services/land-clearing/">lot and brush
        clearing</a>, and <a href="/services/tree-health/">tree health
        work</a> for pines under beetle pressure and oaks in decline.
      </p>

      <h2>What to Ask Any Tree Service Before You Hire One</h2>
      <p>
        Texas does not license tree companies. There is no state test, no
        registry, and nothing stopping anyone with a truck from taking a
        deposit on work they cannot do safely. So ask three questions before
        you hire anyone, including us. Ask for the price in writing. Ask for
        proof of liability insurance. Ask what standard the trimming follows.
        A good company answers all three without blinking, and the
        {' '}<a href="https://tfsweb.tamu.edu/" rel="noopener">Texas A&amp;M
        Forest Service</a> publishes solid guidance on hiring tree care
        professionals if you want a neutral source.
      </p>
      <p>
        You will get our answers on the first call: the estimate is free and
        written, insurance documentation comes with it on request, and
        trimming follows the ANSI A300 standard, which spells out where cuts
        belong and how much live canopy a healthy trim takes.
      </p>

      <h2>Where We Work</h2>
      <p>
        The service area is {b.address.city} and the towns around it in
        Montgomery County: Willis, Montgomery and the Lake Conroe shoreline,
        Panorama Village, Cut and Shoot, and Grangerland. If you are close to
        that footprint but not in it, call anyway. The honest answer might be
        a referral, but you will get it in one phone call instead of three.
      </p>

      <!-- [PENDING — real crew and equipment photos from the operating
           crew, with descriptive filenames + specific alt text, once the
           Conroe operator is signed. No stock, no AI imagery (Rule 29). -->
    </div>
  </section>

  <MapNAP />
  <CTA />
</BaseLayout>
